### What is the Merge Intervals problem?

The problem gives a list of intervals, each with a start and end, and asks to collapse all overlapping ones into a minimal set of non-overlapping intervals that cover the same ranges. This is the foundational problem of the merge-intervals pattern.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class MergeIntervalsService {
    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {
        if (intervals.length <= 1) return intervals;
        Arrays.sort(intervals, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));

        List<int[]> merged = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] current = intervals[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {
            if (intervals[i][0] <= current[1]) {
                current[1] = Math.max(current[1], intervals[i][1]);
            } else {
                merged.add(current);
                current = intervals[i];
            }
        }
        merged.add(current);
        return merged.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] locks = {{9,12},{11,13},{14,16},{15,18},{20,22}};
        int[][] result = new MergeIntervalsService().merge(locks);
        System.out.println("Merged lock windows:");
        for (int[] r : result) System.out.println("  [" + r[0] + ", " + r[1] + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
Merged lock windows:
  [9, 13]
  [14, 18]
  [20, 22]
```

### Why does sorting by start make overlap detection local?

Once intervals are in start order, any interval that overlaps the current merged interval must be its immediate successor or a later one that also overlaps. More importantly, if the next interval does not overlap the current one, no later interval can either (because all later intervals have even larger starts). So we only compare each interval to the single current merged interval.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LocalOverlapExplanation {
    // Sorted: [1,3] [2,6] [8,10]
    // [2,6] overlaps [1,3] because 2 <= 3
    // [8,10] does NOT overlap [1,6] because 8 > 6
    // Since 8 > 6, no later interval can overlap [1,6] either
    // (all later starts >= 8 > 6)

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("After sorting, if next.start > current.end,");
        System.out.println("all subsequent starts are even larger => no overlap.");
    }
}
```

### How do you handle the max-end rule for containment?

When merging two overlapping intervals, the merged end is Math.max(currentEnd, nextEnd). This handles the case where the next interval is fully contained within the current one. Without max, you would shrink the current interval.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MaxEndContainment {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Current: [1, 10], Next: [3, 5] (fully contained)
        // WRONG: current[1] = 5 (shrank from 10 to 5)
        // CORRECT: current[1] = Math.max(10, 5) = 10

        int currentEnd = 10;
        int nextEnd = 5;
        System.out.println("Wrong: " + nextEnd);
        System.out.println("Correct: " + Math.max(currentEnd, nextEnd));
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong: 5
Correct: 10
```

### How do you build a complete banking mini-project?

At National Bank of India, the account-lock service records when exclusive locks are held on an account. Multiple operations can lock the same account at overlapping times. The regulator needs the actual total time an account was under any lock, so the service merges overlapping windows.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class AccountLockMerger {
    record LockWindow(int startHour, int endHour) {}

    public List<LockWindow> mergeLocks(List<LockWindow> windows) {
        if (windows.size() <= 1) return windows;
        windows.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(LockWindow::startHour));

        List<LockWindow> merged = new ArrayList<>();
        int curStart = windows.get(0).startHour();
        int curEnd = windows.get(0).endHour();

        for (int i = 1; i < windows.size(); i++) {
            LockWindow w = windows.get(i);
            if (w.startHour() <= curEnd) {
                curEnd = Math.max(curEnd, w.endHour());
            } else {
                merged.add(new LockWindow(curStart, curEnd));
                curStart = w.startHour();
                curEnd = w.endHour();
            }
        }
        merged.add(new LockWindow(curStart, curEnd));
        return merged;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<LockWindow> windows = List.of(
            new LockWindow(9, 11), new LockWindow(10, 13),
            new LockWindow(15, 17), new LockWindow(16, 19)
        );
        List<LockWindow> merged = new AccountLockMerger().mergeLocks(new ArrayList<>(windows));
        System.out.println("Merged locks:");
        for (LockWindow w : merged) {
            System.out.println("  [" + w.startHour() + ", " + w.endHour() + "]");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Merged locks:
  [9, 13]
  [15, 19]
```

### How is this different from the overlap-detection variant?

| Feature | Merge Intervals | Detect Any Overlap |
|---|---|---|
| Goal | produce merged list | boolean: any overlap? |
| Output | list of merged intervals | true or false |
| Early exit | no, must process all | yes, on first overlap |
| Post-sort work | extend and flush | one adjacent check |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MergeVsDetect {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Merge: produces merged list, processes all intervals.");
        System.out.println("Detect: returns boolean, exits on first overlap.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single interval, all intervals identical, intervals already non-overlapping, and an interval of zero length (start equals end).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class MergeEdgeCasesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MergeIntervalsService solver = new MergeIntervalsService();

        // All identical
        int[][] r = solver.merge(new int[][]{{5,10},{5,10},{5,10}});
        System.out.println("All identical: [" + r[0][0] + "," + r[0][1] + "]");

        // Zero-length interval
        r = solver.merge(new int[][]{{1,1},{2,2}});
        System.out.println("Zero-length: " + r.length + " intervals");
    }
}
```

```text
All identical: [5,10]
Zero-length: 2 intervals
```

### What are common mistakes?

The two correctness traps are forgetting Math.max on the end (shrinks on containment) and forgetting to add the final interval after the loop.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: shrinks on containment
current[1] = intervals[i][1];
// WRONG: forgets final flush
// (no result.add(current) after loop)
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: max-end and final flush
current[1] = Math.max(current[1], intervals[i][1]);
// ... after loop:
result.add(current);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows this is the foundation of the interval-problems family. They use `Instant.compareTo` for real timestamps and `BigDecimal.compareTo` for monetary ranges. They also know the sort is the bottleneck at O(n log n) and the sweep is O(n), and that the space is O(n) for the result or O(1) extra if sorting in place.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorMergeIntervalNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Foundation of all interval problems.");
        System.out.println("O(n log n) sort + O(n) sweep.");
        System.out.println("Use Instant.compareTo for timestamps.");
        System.out.println("Two traps: max-end rule, final flush.");
    }
}
```
